WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.540
Socket I/O is not the same as reading or writing a complete object in one call.

2
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:11.240
Reads and writes may complete partially, block, fail, or be interrupted.

3
00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:16.860
The program must check the actual number of bytes received or sent and update connection state accordingly.

4
00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:24.260
A partial read might be the first bytes of a header, the middle of a payload, or the end of one message plus the beginning of another.

5
00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:33.160
A partial write means the program still has unsent data that must be queued, retried according to policy, or abandoned during failure handling.

6
00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:37.980
Treating partial I/O as rare creates subtle protocol bugs.

7
00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:42.420
Buffers need explicit capacity tracking and clear ownership.

8
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.320
Capacity is how much memory is available.

9
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:49.660
Valid data length is how many bytes have actually been received or prepared.

10
00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.800
Parser position is where the next field begins.

11
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:58.580
These are different facts, and mixing them can lead to incorrect parsing or memory safety mistakes.

12
00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:11.260
Binary data received from a socket should not be treated as a C string unless the protocol explicitly defines text, the bytes are valid for that expectation, and termination is handled safely.

13
00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:17.040
A network message may contain zeros, partial encodings, or no terminator at all.

14
00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:21.540
Create a bounded, validated text representation when text is needed.

15
00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:27.580
Nonblocking and event-driven code must preserve parser state across multiple I/O events.

16
00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:34.560
The parser may need to remember that it has received a partial header, a pending payload, or unsent output.

17
00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:41.940
Safe socket code treats partial I/O as normal behavior and designs state machines that remain clear under repeated events.
